  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">USAID-DOJ ICITAP Wildlife Protection Activity (WPA)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Bangladesh):  Bangladesh is an important source, consumer, and transit point for illegally traded wildlife. The Interagency Agreement (IAA) between USAID and the U.S. Department of Justice (DOJ) International Criminal Investigative Technical Assistance Program capitalizes on the DOJ&apos;s unique relationship with the law enforcement bodies responsible for investigating wildlife crimes, and the judiciary responsible for trying criminals. This IAA improves the status of endangered and protected wildlife species by reducing illegal capture, killing, trade, and consumption of wildlife through strengthening the GoB&apos;s enforcement, investigative capacity, and interagency coordination. ICITAP will build the technical capacity of police; forestry, environment, fisheries, and customs authorities and personnel; as well as border guards and coast guards. With FY 2024 funds, ICITAP will coordinate with USAID&apos;s Wildlife Protection activity to provide training on wildlife crime investigations, analysis, detection, prosecution, and awareness. ICITAP may also purchase equipment to enhance the GoB&apos;s forensics laboratory operations, field-level investigative procedures, as well as improve policy integration and enhance interagency coordination and provide training in the use and care of that equipment.This IAA aligns with the Administration&apos;s Executive Order on transnational crimes and with goal one of the State-USAID Joint Strategic Plan and pillar three of the Indo-Pacific Strategy to strengthen natural resource safeguards and security. Funding for this activity contributes to the required Combating Wildlife Trafficking ($1m) and Transnational Organized Crime ($400k) key issues.</narrative>
  </description>
